<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kashi</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 550 BCE (approx.) · capital Varanasi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient kingdom of Varanasi on the Ganges, an early centre of Vedic learning and a prize fought over by Kosala and Magadha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kashi grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varuna-river">Varuna</a> meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, in the Later Vedic period. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brihadaranyaka-upanishad">Brihadaranyaka Upanishad</a> names a learned king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru-of-kashi">Ajatashatru of Kashi</a>, who taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> Gargya about the nature of the self. By the 6th century BCE it was listed among the sixteen mahajanapadas of Buddhist texts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its wealth came from river trade, fine textiles and its sacred standing, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> was already a place of pilgrimage and teaching. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jataka-tales">Jataka tales</a> set many stories in the reigns of kings called Brahmadatta of Varanasi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> tradition makes the 23rd tirthankara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a>, the son of a Kashi king. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a> gave his first sermon at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kashi lost a long rivalry with its neighbour, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kosala">Kingdom of Kosala</a>, in the 6th century BCE. Revenue from a Kashi village formed the dowry of a Kosalan princess who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara of Magadha</a>, and the quarrel over it led to war; the territory passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism's</a> holiest city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kashi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kashi" width="320" height="246"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahajanapadas_(c._500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 550 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Varanasi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ajatashatru of Kashi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vedic Sanskrit, Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kashi, Kasi Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-675&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kashi" aria-label="Kingdom of Kashi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6879 1246 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kashi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7199 1502L7155 1425"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7155 1425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7199 1502h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7199 1502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Varanasi. <a href="/atlas?year=-675&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kashi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

